WEBVTT

1
00:00:17.346 --> 00:00:21.367
Skyler surveyed the clean beach and let out a sigh of relief.

2
00:00:23.468 --> 00:00:26.129
Even somber Walter showed a hint of a smile.

3
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.952
Skyler had helped to organize a beach cleanup for her school's Green Club.

4
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:40.542
She had only recently joined the club, after gaining an interest in environmental issues, and eagerly volunteered to

5
00:00:40.622 --> 00:00:41.642
help plan the effort.

6
00:00:43.463 --> 00:00:47.104
The Green Club had teamed up with a local private school for the first time.

7
00:00:48.864 --> 00:00:54.546
Skyler was definitely impressed by Walter, the president of the private school's environmental club.

8
00:00:56.806 --> 00:01:03.609
Walter had not only gotten plastic utensils and straws banned from his school's cafeteria, but he'd also gotten

9
00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:04.750
the two schools together.

10
00:01:07.252 --> 00:01:14.178
But there was one thing about working with Walter that was challenging—he was always so serious!

11
00:01:14.198 --> 00:01:21.484
"This is going to look great for our yearbook," Skyler said excitedly, snapping a picture of the beach.

12
00:01:21.504 --> 00:01:27.329
"It was a long morning, but we cleaned up the entire beach!

13
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.012
Now it looks almost pristine."

14
00:01:34.016 --> 00:01:38.798
"We did a lot of work, but there's still plastic all around," Walter said.

15
00:01:38.818 --> 00:01:43.060
"Huh?" Skyler furrowed her brow.

16
00:01:44.561 --> 00:01:50.163
The students had collected a huge amount of plastic debris—wrappers, bottles, and bags.

17
00:01:51.824 --> 00:01:57.446
Club members were now carefully combing through it, checking to see what was recyclable and what was not.

18
00:01:57.466 --> 00:02:01.988
"I don't see anymore plastic," she mumbled.

19
00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:10.085
"You might not see it, but that doesn't mean it's not there," Walter said.

20
00:02:10.105 --> 00:02:13.966
"Haven't you heard of microplastics?"

21
00:02:15.727 --> 00:02:17.307
"Oh!" Skyler said.

22
00:02:17.327 --> 00:02:22.229
"I've heard of microplastics, but I don't really know much about them."

23
00:02:22.249 --> 00:02:28.210
"Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic less than five millimeters in size.

24
00:02:29.831 --> 00:02:33.872
Looking at the beach now, it's not obvious that it's still polluted with plastic.

25
00:02:35.468 --> 00:02:37.869
That's because microplastics are so tiny."

26
00:02:37.889 --> 00:02:42.512
"Where do microplastics come from?"

27
00:02:42.972 --> 00:02:43.833
Skyler asked.

28
00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:49.236
"They come from nearly anything that has plastic," Walter said.

29
00:02:50.657 --> 00:02:55.520
"Even though plastic may take hundreds of years to break down—which is why we need to lessen our

30
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.061
consumption—it still breaks down.

31
00:02:59.302 --> 00:03:02.964
And when it does, tiny fragments are released into the water, soil, and air."

32
00:03:04.276 --> 00:03:07.338
"Air?" "Yeah.

33
00:03:07.758 --> 00:03:11.660
Microplastics are even in the air, which means we breathe them in.

34
00:03:13.481 --> 00:03:19.044
Since the particles are so small, they can easily travel through the air like dust.

35
00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:23.086
Or they come from synthetic fibers like polyester that get shed from clothes.

36
00:03:24.647 --> 00:03:27.729
These fibers also enter the water when we do laundry."

37
00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:29.490
Walter sighed.

38
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:35.153
"Did you know that microplastics are in the food chain too?"

39
00:03:37.375 --> 00:03:38.536
Skyler shook her head.

40
00:03:38.556 --> 00:03:44.240
"A lot of microplastics get consumed by seafood," Walter continued.

41
00:03:45.701 --> 00:03:50.365
"Small sea animals ingest microplastics, then larger fish eat them.

42
00:03:51.706 --> 00:03:53.287
And then we eat those fish!

43
00:03:54.468 --> 00:03:59.892
What's worse is that the chemicals used to make plastic can be toxic and even cause cancer.

44
00:04:01.244 --> 00:04:03.746
All this stuff ends up in our bodies."

45
00:04:03.766 --> 00:04:12.572
"I thought I knew a lot about saving the planet," Skyler said, "but I guess there's still a

46
00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:13.493
lot to learn."

47
00:04:14.834 --> 00:04:15.654
Walter nodded.

48
00:04:15.674 --> 00:04:20.477
"Microplastics aren't new, but our understanding of them is.

49
00:04:21.938 --> 00:04:25.161
And many people still don't see how prevalent the problem really is."

50
00:04:25.181 --> 00:04:29.544
"I wonder what can be done," Skyler said.

51
00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.482
"Well, cleaning up the beach was a good thing to do," Walter said.

52
00:04:35.502 --> 00:04:40.665
"It will prevent some microplastics from contaminating the beach.

53
00:04:41.926 --> 00:04:45.307
We should all recycle, of course, and try to use less plastic.

54
00:04:46.588 --> 00:04:48.989
Or, we can use biodegradable plastic instead.

55
00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.432
New plastics are being made out of biodegradable materials."

56
00:04:54.852 --> 00:04:59.996
"But they might not be as biodegradable as we think, and some use more materials to make than

57
00:05:00.056 --> 00:05:02.198
regular plastics," Skyler said.

58
00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:10.525
"It's also easier for wealthy people to use less plastic or buy different products, but not everyone can

59
00:05:10.565 --> 00:05:11.626
afford to do that.

60
00:05:13.307 --> 00:05:15.509
Green products are usually more expensive.

61
00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.814
It's hard to tell people to use less plastic when the alternatives are too expensive for them."

62
00:05:21.834 --> 00:05:27.362
"Right, and some products labeled 'green' aren't really green at all.

63
00:05:28.563 --> 00:05:33.707
Companies just use green packaging or earth-friendly words to sell things at higher prices."

64
00:05:33.727 --> 00:05:38.090
"That's called greenwashing."

65
00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.031
Walter nodded.

66
00:05:40.051 --> 00:05:46.275
"Yeah. I convinced my mom to use new floor wipes because the box looked eco-friendly.

67
00:05:47.596 --> 00:05:50.498
But later I read that they weren't any different from the other wipes.

68
00:05:52.019 --> 00:05:53.180
That made me mad."

69
00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.580
He shook his head.

70
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.541
"Saving the earth is the right thing for all of us to do.

71
00:06:00.602 --> 00:06:04.203
But lots of corporations treat it as a way to make more money."

72
00:06:06.383 --> 00:06:09.504
"Our planet is running out of time," Skyler said.

73
00:06:09.524 --> 00:06:13.866
"I hope we can find some solutions before it's too late."

74
00:06:14.986 --> 00:06:19.227
Walter glanced up at the other kids who were still busy sorting plastic.

75
00:06:20.407 --> 00:06:22.348
"There aren't any easy solutions.

76
00:06:23.756 --> 00:06:27.678
But you and I have joined these clubs, and today's project will make a difference.

77
00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:30.740
And right now there's something else we can do."

78
00:06:33.021 --> 00:06:33.842
Walter grinned.

79
00:06:33.862 --> 00:06:36.143
"Help the other kids!

80
00:06:36.463 --> 00:06:38.284
They probably think we're slacking off!"

81
00:06:39.605 --> 00:06:43.247
Skyler smiled back, pleased to see Walter lightening up.

82
00:06:44.447 --> 00:06:47.969
As they headed toward the group of students, Walter suddenly stopped.

83
00:06:47.989 --> 00:06:52.692
"Maybe our school clubs can collaborate more often," he said.

84
00:06:53.808 --> 00:06:55.352
Skyler's smile widened.

85
00:06:55.372 --> 00:06:57.638
"Great idea!"
